{"type":"page","html":"<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><meta charSet=\"utf-8\" data-next-head=\"\"/><meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width\" data-next-head=\"\"/><title data-next-head=\"\">M.E ESONANJOR and Company | WHERE LEGAL STRATEGY IS PRECISION-BUILT.</title><meta name=\"description\" content=\"M.E ESONANJOR and Company offers top-tier legal services across multiple practice areas including Corporate Law, Real Estate, and Litigation. Tailored legal solutions for your business and personal needs.\" data-next-head=\"\"/><meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"website\" data-next-head=\"\"/><meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"M.E ESONANJOR and Company | WHERE LEGAL STRATEGY IS PRECISION-BUILT.\" data-next-head=\"\"/><meta property=\"og:description\" content=\"M.E ESONANJOR and Company offers expert legal services across Corporate Law, Real Estate, Litigation, and more. Get tailored legal solutions today.\" data-next-head=\"\"/><meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://m-e-esonanjor-and-company.vercel.app/hero.png\" data-next-head=\"\"/><meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://m-e-esonanjor-and-company.vercel.app\" data-next-head=\"\"/><meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"M.E ESONANJOR and Company\" data-next-head=\"\"/><meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\" data-next-head=\"\"/><meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"M.E ESONANJOR and Company | WHERE LEGAL STRATEGY IS PRECISION-BUILT.\" data-next-head=\"\"/><meta name=\"twitter:description\" content=\"M.E ESONANJOR and Company offers expert legal services in various fields, providing clients with solutions tailored to their needs.\" data-next-head=\"\"/><meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://m-e-esonanjor-and-company.vercel.app/hero.png\" data-next-head=\"\"/><meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@MEESONANJOR\" data-next-head=\"\"/><link rel=\"icon\" href=\"/logo.png\" data-next-head=\"\"/><meta name=\"robots\" content=\"index, follow\" data-next-head=\"\"/><link data-next-font=\"size-adjust\" rel=\"preconnect\" href=\"/\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\"/><link rel=\"preload\" href=\"/_next/static/css/559f3cc741d6c5f3.css\" as=\"style\"/><link href=\"https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=EB+Garamond&amp;display=swap\" rel=\"stylesheet\" data-next-head=\"\"/><script type=\"application/ld+json\" data-next-head=\"\">{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"LegalService\",\"name\":\"M.E ESONANJOR and Company\",\"url\":\"https://m-e-esonanjor-and-company.vercel.app\",\"logo\":\"https://m-e-esonanjor-and-company.vercel.app/logo.png\",\"description\":\"WHERE LEGAL STRATEGY IS PRECISION-BUILT.\",\"address\":{\"@type\":\"PostalAddress\",\"addressLocality\":\"Lagos\",\"addressCountry\":\"Nigeria\",\"postalCode\":\"101001\"},\"sameAs\":[\"https://twitter.com/M.E ESONANJOR and Company\",\"https://www.linkedin.com/company/m-e-esonanjor-and-company\",\"https://www.facebook.com/m-e-esonanjor-and-company\"]}</script><link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"/_next/static/css/559f3cc741d6c5f3.css\" data-n-g=\"\"/><noscript data-n-css=\"\"></noscript><script defer=\"\" noModule=\"\" src=\"/_next/static/chunks/polyfills-42372ed130431b0a.js\"></script><script src=\"/_next/static/chunks/webpack-06833fb297db238c.js\" defer=\"\"></script><script src=\"/_next/static/chunks/framework-86ad0b4475c628a3.js\" defer=\"\"></script><script src=\"/_next/static/chunks/main-017b46be1b747284.js\" defer=\"\"></script><script src=\"/_next/static/chunks/pages/_app-97f6a6cf27a46b5f.js\" defer=\"\"></script><script src=\"/_next/static/chunks/311-65e3d4591f3dc1f3.js\" defer=\"\"></script><script src=\"/_next/static/chunks/606-45605b30cc1f5f27.js\" defer=\"\"></script><script src=\"/_next/static/chunks/727-02c45049c9645fd6.js\" defer=\"\"></script><script src=\"/_next/static/chunks/pages/blog/%5Bslug%5D-02876a39f5531461.js\" defer=\"\"></script><script src=\"/_next/static/C8UV40mM35vZv8dfZSkeF/_buildManifest.js\" defer=\"\"></script><script src=\"/_next/static/C8UV40mM35vZv8dfZSkeF/_ssgManifest.js\" defer=\"\"></script></head><body><link rel=\"preload\" as=\"image\" href=\"/logo.png\"/><div id=\"__next\"><div><header class=\"bg-gray-800 sticky top-0 z-20 text-white py-6 px-8 shadow-lg\"><div class=\"max-w-7xl mx-auto flex justify-between items-center\"><a class=\"flex items-center space-x-3\" href=\"/\"><img src=\"/logo.png\" alt=\"Honoredge Legal Practice Logo\" class=\"h-14 w-auto\"/></a><nav class=\"hidden md:flex space-x-8\"><a class=\"relative group font-semibold tracking-wide\" href=\"/about\">About<span class=\"absolute left-0 -bottom-1 w-0 h-0.5 bg-white transition-all group-hover:w-full\"></span></a><a class=\"relative group font-semibold tracking-wide\" href=\"/attorneys\">Attorneys<span class=\"absolute left-0 -bottom-1 w-0 h-0.5 bg-white transition-all group-hover:w-full\"></span></a><a class=\"relative group font-semibold tracking-wide\" href=\"/#practice-areas\">Practice Areas<span class=\"absolute left-0 -bottom-1 w-0 h-0.5 bg-white transition-all group-hover:w-full\"></span></a><a class=\"relative group font-semibold tracking-wide\" href=\"/blog\">Blog<span class=\"absolute left-0 -bottom-1 w-0 h-0.5 bg-white transition-all group-hover:w-full\"></span></a><a class=\"relative group font-semibold tracking-wide\" href=\"/contact\">Contact<span class=\"absolute left-0 -bottom-1 w-0 h-0.5 bg-white transition-all group-hover:w-full\"></span></a></nav><button class=\"md:hidden text-white focus:outline-none\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"28\" height=\"28\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-menu\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><line x1=\"4\" x2=\"20\" y1=\"12\" y2=\"12\"></line><line x1=\"4\" x2=\"20\" y1=\"6\" y2=\"6\"></line><line x1=\"4\" x2=\"20\" y1=\"18\" y2=\"18\"></line></svg></button></div></header><main><div class=\"max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10\"><h1 class=\"text-4xl font-bold mb-4\">Union Homes License Revocation Article</h1><div class=\"mt-6\"><h1 class=\"text-3xl font-bold my-4\"><strong class=\"font-semibold\">CBN Revokes Union Homes Savings &amp; Loans PLC License:<br/><br/>What Depositors and Stakeholders Need to Know<br/></strong></h1><p class=\"my-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed\">The Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) has exercised its regulatory authority under Section 12(1) of the Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act 2020 to revoke the operating license of Union Homes Savings &amp; Loans PLC. This action marks the formal cessation of the institution’s banking operations and triggers a structured liquidation process managed by the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC).</p><p class=\"my-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed\"><br/>As Nigeria’s apex financial regulator, the CBN is charged with maintaining systemic stability, protecting depositors, and ensuring the integrity of the banking sector. License revocation represents one of the most serious enforcement actions available under Nigerian banking law, typically reserved for institutions facing critical financial distress with no viable path to recovery.</p><p class=\"my-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed\"></p><p class=\"my-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed\"><br/><strong class=\"font-semibold\">1. Legal Framework and Regulatory Authority</strong><br/>Section 12 of BOFIA 2020 grants the CBN broad powers to revoke banking licenses where financial institutions demonstrate persistent inability to meet regulatory requirements. The decision to revoke Union Homes’ license reflects the CBN’s determination that the institution’s financial challenges have reached a critical threshold with no reasonable prospect of remediation.</p><p class=\"my-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed\"><br/><strong class=\"font-semibold\">Common Grounds for License Revocation</strong><br/>The CBN may revoke a financial institution’s license based on any of the following circumstances:<br/>•Severe undercapitalization – failure to maintain minimum capital adequacy ratios required by the CBN<br/>•Insolvency or technical insolvency – liabilities exceeding assets with no recovery prospects<br/>•Persistent regulatory non-compliance – repeated failure to submit returns or comply with CBN directives<br/>•Unsafe or unsound banking practices – operations that jeopardize financial system stability<br/>•Prolonged operational inactivity – cessation of core banking activities for six months or more<br/>•Breach of licensing conditions – failure to fulfill obligations imposed when the license was granted</p><p class=\"my-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed\"><br/><strong class=\"font-semibold\">2. Immediate Actions for Depositors</strong></p><p class=\"my-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed\"><br/><strong class=\"font-semibold\">NDIC Liquidation Process</strong><br/>Following license revocation, the NDIC automatically assumes the role of liquidator pursuant to Section 12(2) of BOFIA 2020 and Section 40 of the NDIC Act. The NDIC exercises all powers granted to liquidators under the Companies and Allied Matters Act (CAMA) and operates with a statutory mandate to protect depositors’ interests.<br/></p><p class=\"my-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed\"><strong class=\"font-semibold\">Claims Settlement Priority</strong><br/>The NDIC processes claims according to the following hierarchy:<br/>1.Depositors – receive first priority in asset distribution<br/>2.General creditors and investors – paid after depositor claims are satisfied<br/>3.Shareholders – last in priority, typically receiving no recovery</p><p class=\"my-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed\"><br/><strong class=\"font-semibold\">Deposit Insurance Coverage</strong><br/>The NDIC provides deposit insurance coverage up to a maximum of N5,000,000 per depositor. This protection applies to all eligible deposit accounts held at Union Homes as of the revocation date.<br/>Key timelines:<br/>•Insured deposits (up to N5 million): paid within 30 days of account verification<br/>•Uninsured deposits (amounts exceeding N5 million): paid through liquidation dividends as assets are realized and debts recovered<br/>Required Documentation<br/>To facilitate verification and payment, depositors must provide:<br/>•Valid government-issued identification (national ID, driver’s license, international passport)<br/>•Proof of account ownership (bank statements, passbook, account opening documents)<br/>•Alternative bank account details for electronic fund transfers<br/>•Completed NDIC verification forms (distributed during the verification exercise)<br/>Joint Account Treatment</p><p class=\"my-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed\"><br/>Where depositors hold both individual and joint accounts, each account receives separate insurance coverage up to the N5 million maximum. For example, if two spouses each maintain individual accounts and also hold a joint account, all three accounts qualify for independent protection, potentially yielding total coverage of N15 million.</p><p class=\"my-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed\"><br/><strong class=\"font-semibold\">3. Options for the Affected Institution</strong><br/>While license revocation represents a terminal regulatory action, affected institutions retain limited recourse options:</p><p class=\"my-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed\"><br/><strong class=\"font-semibold\">Judicial Review</strong><br/>Union Homes retains the right to seek judicial review of the CBN’s decision before the Federal High Court. However, Sections 12(4), (5), and (6) of BOFIA 2020 impose significant limitations on judicial intervention. Courts cannot compel the CBN to reinstate a revoked license, and judicial review typically focuses on procedural propriety rather than the substantive merits of the CBN’s assessment.<br/>License Reapplication<br/>In cases not involving fraud or gross mismanagement, an institution may theoretically reapply for a banking license after satisfying all deficiencies that led to the initial revocation. However, given the capital requirements and operational standards imposed by current banking regulations, successful reapplication following revocation remains exceptionally rare. Institutions may alternatively seek a downgraded license for more limited financial services operations.</p><p class=\"my-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed\"><br/><strong class=\"font-semibold\">4. Impact on Investors and Shareholders</strong><br/>Equity investors and shareholders of Union Homes face severe consequences under the liquidation framework:<br/>Complete Loss of Investment<br/>Shareholders rank last in the claims hierarchy established by Section 2(c) of the NDIC Act. In virtually all bank liquidations, asset realization proves insufficient to satisfy depositor and creditor claims, leaving nothing for distribution to shareholders. Equity investments should be considered total losses upon license revocation.<br/>Loss of Corporate Control<br/>Upon appointment as liquidator, the NDIC assumes full control over the institution’s management, assets, and operations pursuant to Section 585(9) of CAMA. The existing board of directors and management lose all decision-making authority, and shareholders cannot influence the liquidation process.<br/>No Interim Distributions<br/>Unlike depositors who receive prompt payment of insured amounts, shareholders cannot expect any interim distributions during the liquidation process. Only after complete satisfaction of all priority claims—which may take several years—would shareholders become eligible for residual distributions, which historically have proven nonexistent in failed bank liquidations.</p><p class=\"my-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed\"><br/><strong class=\"font-semibold\">5. Broader Financial System Implications</strong><br/>License revocations generate ripple effects throughout the banking sector that extend beyond the immediate stakeholders of the failed institution:</p><p class=\"my-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed\"><br/><strong class=\"font-semibold\">Confidence and Contagion Risk</strong><br/>High-profile bank failures can erode public confidence in the broader financial system, particularly among depositors of smaller or mid-tier institutions. The CBN’s decisive action, while necessary for systemic stability, may temporarily increase deposit flight toward larger, perceived-safer institutions.</p><p class=\"my-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed\"><br/><strong class=\"font-semibold\">Credit Market Effects</strong><br/>Surviving banks may adopt more conservative lending practices following a peer institution’s failure, potentially constraining credit availability for businesses and consumers. Interbank lending rates may also experience short-term volatility as institutions reassess counterparty risk.</p><p class=\"my-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed\"><br/><strong class=\"font-semibold\">Regulatory Precedent</strong><br/>The CBN’s willingness to revoke licenses reinforces its commitment to maintaining banking sector integrity regardless of political or economic pressures. This regulatory assertiveness, while disruptive in individual cases, ultimately strengthens the long-term resilience and credibility of Nigeria’s financial system.</p><p class=\"my-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed\"><br/><strong class=\"font-semibold\">Conclusion</strong><br/>The revocation of Union Homes Savings &amp; Loans PLC’s license represents a necessary, albeit painful, exercise of regulatory authority to protect depositors and maintain financial system stability. While depositors benefit from the NDIC’s insurance protection and structured liquidation process, shareholders and unsecured creditors face substantial losses.<br/>The CBN’s actions underscore the critical importance of continuous regulatory compliance and sound financial management in the banking sector. Institutions operating at the margins of regulatory requirements must recognize that the CBN possesses both the authority and willingness to intervene decisively when financial stability is threatened.<br/>For stakeholders affected by this revocation, timely engagement with the NDIC verification process remains essential to securing available protections and recoveries.<br/><br/><em class=\"italic\">This article is intended for informational purposes only and does not constitute legal advice. For specific guidance on matters related to Union Homes Savings &amp; Loans PLC or other banking regulatory issues, please contact Abayomi Arole Legal Practitioners.</em></p></div><div class=\"flex items-center mt-6 mb-10 space-x-4\"><div><h3 class=\"text-lg font-semibold\"></h3></div></div></div></main><footer class=\"bg-gray-800 text-white py-16 px-6 md:px-20\"><div class=\"max-w-7xl mx-auto grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-12\"><div class=\"flex flex-col items-center md:items-start\"><img alt=\" M.E ESONANJOR and Company Logo\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"100\" height=\"100\" decoding=\"async\" data-nimg=\"1\" class=\"object-contain\" style=\"color:transparent\" srcSet=\"/_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Flogo.6cd93c1f.png&amp;w=128&amp;q=75 1x, /_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Flogo.6cd93c1f.png&amp;w=256&amp;q=75 2x\" src=\"/_next/image?url=%2F_next%2Fstatic%2Fmedia%2Flogo.6cd93c1f.png&amp;w=256&amp;q=75\"/><h3 class=\"mt-4 text-2xl font-bold font-garamond tracking-wider leading-tight text-center md:text-left uppercase\">M.E ESONANJOR and Company</h3></div><div class=\"flex flex-col space-y-2\"><h4 class=\"text-2xl font-garamond mb-4\">Contact Details</h4><address class=\"not-italic text-lg\">34 Oduduwa Way,<br/>GRA, Ikeja,<br/>Lagos, Nigeria.</address><p class=\"mt-4 text-lg\">Phone:<!-- --> <a href=\"tel:+2347048828395\" class=\"hover:underline\">+234 704 882 8395</a><br/><a href=\"tel:+2348033818650\" class=\"hover:underline\">+234 803 381 8650</a></p><p class=\"text-lg\">Email:<!-- --> <a href=\"mailto:attorney@mesonanjor.com\" class=\"hover:underline\">attorney@mesonanjor.com</a></p></div><div class=\"flex flex-col space-y-2\"><h4 class=\"text-2xl font-garamond mb-4\">Office Hours</h4><div class=\"text-lg\"><p>Monday to Friday: 8:00 AM – 5:30 PM</p><p>Saturday: Closed</p><p>Sunday: Closed</p></div></div></div><div class=\"max-w-7xl mx-auto mt-12 border-t border-gray-500 pt-8 text-center md:text-left\"><div class=\"flex flex-col md:flex-row md:items-center md:justify-between\"><div><h4 class=\"text-xl font-garamond mb-4 md:mb-2\">Follow Us</h4><div class=\"flex justify-center md:justify-start space-x-6 text-2xl\"><a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/share/16LqV19wid/?mibextid=wwXIfr\" aria-label=\"Facebook\" class=\"hover:text-gray-300 focus:text-gray-300\"><i class=\"fab fa-facebook-f\"></i></a><a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/m-e-esonanjor-and-company-1b4035390\" aria-label=\"LinkedIn\" class=\"hover:text-gray-300 focus:text-gray-300\"><i class=\"fab fa-linkedin\"></i></a><a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/m.e_esonanjor.co?igsh=cnExdW1waTJlYmxk&amp;utm_source=qr\" aria-label=\"Instagram\" class=\"hover:text-gray-300 focus:text-gray-300\"><i class=\"fab fa-instagram\"></i></a></div></div><div class=\"mt-6 md:mt-0 text-sm \">© <!-- -->2026<!-- --> M.E ESONANJOR AND COMPANY. All rights reserved.</div></div></div></footer></div></div><script id=\"__NEXT_DATA__\" type=\"application/json\">{\"props\":{\"pageProps\":{\"post\":{\"_id\":\"1d90a673-1019-4ee1-9541-b99c2256a728\",\"author\":null,\"body\":[{\"_key\":\"700e49436ed3\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"de725318247a\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"CBN Revokes Union Homes Savings \\u0026 Loans PLC License:\\n\\nWhat Depositors and Stakeholders Need to Know\\n\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"h1\"},{\"_key\":\"82ee54a4ce3f\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"538ab9628dc4\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"The Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) has exercised its regulatory authority under Section 12(1) of the Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act 2020 to revoke the operating license of Union Homes Savings \\u0026 Loans PLC. This action marks the formal cessation of the institution’s banking operations and triggers a structured liquidation process managed by the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC).\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"d1eef88d1aaa\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"489f0ae70456\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\nAs Nigeria’s apex financial regulator, the CBN is charged with maintaining systemic stability, protecting depositors, and ensuring the integrity of the banking sector. License revocation represents one of the most serious enforcement actions available under Nigerian banking law, typically reserved for institutions facing critical financial distress with no viable path to recovery.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"c7ccaab3c66c\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"367ebc54aaf0\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"33819de68013\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"79db972d00ee\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"c54469b3b17a\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"1. Legal Framework and Regulatory Authority\"},{\"_key\":\"fc59fbcc8f53\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\nSection 12 of BOFIA 2020 grants the CBN broad powers to revoke banking licenses where financial institutions demonstrate persistent inability to meet regulatory requirements. The decision to revoke Union Homes’ license reflects the CBN’s determination that the institution’s financial challenges have reached a critical threshold with no reasonable prospect of remediation.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"7244737e7f71\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"f3b37432de73\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"014d80bca8dc\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"Common Grounds for License Revocation\"},{\"_key\":\"712e41d0b5a7\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\nThe CBN may revoke a financial institution’s license based on any of the following circumstances:\\n•Severe undercapitalization – failure to maintain minimum capital adequacy ratios required by the CBN\\n•Insolvency or technical insolvency – liabilities exceeding assets with no recovery prospects\\n•Persistent regulatory non-compliance – repeated failure to submit returns or comply with CBN directives\\n•Unsafe or unsound banking practices – operations that jeopardize financial system stability\\n•Prolonged operational inactivity – cessation of core banking activities for six months or more\\n•Breach of licensing conditions – failure to fulfill obligations imposed when the license was granted\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"2252a2d55b30\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"5cb9cb40f152\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"67fa42e8de09\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"2. Immediate Actions for Depositors\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"408b7dd3a790\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"dbf591dc91a0\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"3d7c36f7fdcd\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"NDIC Liquidation Process\"},{\"_key\":\"d39635d28345\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\nFollowing license revocation, the NDIC automatically assumes the role of liquidator pursuant to Section 12(2) of BOFIA 2020 and Section 40 of the NDIC Act. The NDIC exercises all powers granted to liquidators under the Companies and Allied Matters Act (CAMA) and operates with a statutory mandate to protect depositors’ interests.\\n\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"c73fada9b746\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"ca95c14f77ec\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"Claims Settlement Priority\"},{\"_key\":\"eb7cb1a69145\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\nThe NDIC processes claims according to the following hierarchy:\\n1.Depositors – receive first priority in asset distribution\\n2.General creditors and investors – paid after depositor claims are satisfied\\n3.Shareholders – last in priority, typically receiving no recovery\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"c7962795721d\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"e009caa565c9\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"4eb56d1a288d\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"Deposit Insurance Coverage\"},{\"_key\":\"b0bb3dbfc346\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\nThe NDIC provides deposit insurance coverage up to a maximum of N5,000,000 per depositor. This protection applies to all eligible deposit accounts held at Union Homes as of the revocation date.\\nKey timelines:\\n•Insured deposits (up to N5 million): paid within 30 days of account verification\\n•Uninsured deposits (amounts exceeding N5 million): paid through liquidation dividends as assets are realized and debts recovered\\nRequired Documentation\\nTo facilitate verification and payment, depositors must provide:\\n•Valid government-issued identification (national ID, driver’s license, international passport)\\n•Proof of account ownership (bank statements, passbook, account opening documents)\\n•Alternative bank account details for electronic fund transfers\\n•Completed NDIC verification forms (distributed during the verification exercise)\\nJoint Account Treatment\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"34e74fe0c8ae\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"3804f3a0eee5\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\nWhere depositors hold both individual and joint accounts, each account receives separate insurance coverage up to the N5 million maximum. For example, if two spouses each maintain individual accounts and also hold a joint account, all three accounts qualify for independent protection, potentially yielding total coverage of N15 million.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"01cf831bf3a9\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"302e57a06925\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"096a02c36b9d\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"3. Options for the Affected Institution\"},{\"_key\":\"09cca4cee258\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\nWhile license revocation represents a terminal regulatory action, affected institutions retain limited recourse options:\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"0daa19bcb6bf\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"ae9786f2c6e4\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"29900db0f496\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"Judicial Review\"},{\"_key\":\"c4dcb2031f35\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\nUnion Homes retains the right to seek judicial review of the CBN’s decision before the Federal High Court. However, Sections 12(4), (5), and (6) of BOFIA 2020 impose significant limitations on judicial intervention. Courts cannot compel the CBN to reinstate a revoked license, and judicial review typically focuses on procedural propriety rather than the substantive merits of the CBN’s assessment.\\nLicense Reapplication\\nIn cases not involving fraud or gross mismanagement, an institution may theoretically reapply for a banking license after satisfying all deficiencies that led to the initial revocation. However, given the capital requirements and operational standards imposed by current banking regulations, successful reapplication following revocation remains exceptionally rare. Institutions may alternatively seek a downgraded license for more limited financial services operations.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"3d664ce3fd8b\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"5be041e907c3\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"49e734de3eda\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"4. Impact on Investors and Shareholders\"},{\"_key\":\"f32c28b5169a\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\nEquity investors and shareholders of Union Homes face severe consequences under the liquidation framework:\\nComplete Loss of Investment\\nShareholders rank last in the claims hierarchy established by Section 2(c) of the NDIC Act. In virtually all bank liquidations, asset realization proves insufficient to satisfy depositor and creditor claims, leaving nothing for distribution to shareholders. Equity investments should be considered total losses upon license revocation.\\nLoss of Corporate Control\\nUpon appointment as liquidator, the NDIC assumes full control over the institution’s management, assets, and operations pursuant to Section 585(9) of CAMA. The existing board of directors and management lose all decision-making authority, and shareholders cannot influence the liquidation process.\\nNo Interim Distributions\\nUnlike depositors who receive prompt payment of insured amounts, shareholders cannot expect any interim distributions during the liquidation process. Only after complete satisfaction of all priority claims—which may take several years—would shareholders become eligible for residual distributions, which historically have proven nonexistent in failed bank liquidations.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"213cb3ccc397\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"4405ca500470\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"61995480070a\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"5. Broader Financial System Implications\"},{\"_key\":\"30f627388510\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\nLicense revocations generate ripple effects throughout the banking sector that extend beyond the immediate stakeholders of the failed institution:\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"cd4bea49f25c\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"6f073a578fde\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"12000c83e375\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"Confidence and Contagion Risk\"},{\"_key\":\"c065841d7a8e\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\nHigh-profile bank failures can erode public confidence in the broader financial system, particularly among depositors of smaller or mid-tier institutions. The CBN’s decisive action, while necessary for systemic stability, may temporarily increase deposit flight toward larger, perceived-safer institutions.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"0cb46a20adb0\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"3d5748f65ac6\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"5cb92ed9e1e4\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"Credit Market Effects\"},{\"_key\":\"8e4668e8cfa8\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\nSurviving banks may adopt more conservative lending practices following a peer institution’s failure, potentially constraining credit availability for businesses and consumers. Interbank lending rates may also experience short-term volatility as institutions reassess counterparty risk.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"96f480e9bdfb\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"cb2af05b69ac\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"70ed3b69db4f\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"Regulatory Precedent\"},{\"_key\":\"b62e1bee7d88\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\nThe CBN’s willingness to revoke licenses reinforces its commitment to maintaining banking sector integrity regardless of political or economic pressures. This regulatory assertiveness, while disruptive in individual cases, ultimately strengthens the long-term resilience and credibility of Nigeria’s financial system.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"},{\"_key\":\"49cf5aeab304\",\"_type\":\"block\",\"children\":[{\"_key\":\"2a8d75d82993\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"c98207dde6dd\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"strong\"],\"text\":\"Conclusion\"},{\"_key\":\"30cd4ed9c26f\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[],\"text\":\"\\nThe revocation of Union Homes Savings \\u0026 Loans PLC’s license represents a necessary, albeit painful, exercise of regulatory authority to protect depositors and maintain financial system stability. While depositors benefit from the NDIC’s insurance protection and structured liquidation process, shareholders and unsecured creditors face substantial losses.\\nThe CBN’s actions underscore the critical importance of continuous regulatory compliance and sound financial management in the banking sector. Institutions operating at the margins of regulatory requirements must recognize that the CBN possesses both the authority and willingness to intervene decisively when financial stability is threatened.\\nFor stakeholders affected by this revocation, timely engagement with the NDIC verification process remains essential to securing available protections and recoveries.\\n\\n\"},{\"_key\":\"91d96542de00\",\"_type\":\"span\",\"marks\":[\"em\"],\"text\":\"This article is intended for informational purposes only and does not constitute legal advice. For specific guidance on matters related to Union Homes Savings \\u0026 Loans PLC or other banking regulatory issues, please contact Abayomi Arole Legal Practitioners.\"}],\"markDefs\":[],\"style\":\"normal\"}],\"comments\":[],\"mainImage\":null,\"title\":\"Union Homes License Revocation Article\"}},\"__N_SSG\":true},\"page\":\"/blog/[slug]\",\"query\":{\"slug\":\"union-homes-license-revocation-article\"},\"buildId\":\"C8UV40mM35vZv8dfZSkeF\",\"isFallback\":false,\"gsp\":true,\"scriptLoader\":[]}</script></body></html>","json":{"pageProps":{"post":{"_id":"1d90a673-1019-4ee1-9541-b99c2256a728","author":null,"body":[{"_key":"700e49436ed3","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"de725318247a","_type":"span","marks":["strong"],"text":"CBN Revokes Union Homes Savings & Loans PLC License:\n\nWhat Depositors and Stakeholders Need to Know\n"}],"markDefs":[],"style":"h1"},{"_key":"82ee54a4ce3f","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"538ab9628dc4","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"The Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) has exercised its regulatory authority under Section 12(1) of the Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act 2020 to revoke the operating license of Union Homes Savings & Loans PLC. This action marks the formal cessation of the institution’s banking operations and triggers a structured liquidation process managed by the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC)."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"d1eef88d1aaa","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"489f0ae70456","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\nAs Nigeria’s apex financial regulator, the CBN is charged with maintaining systemic stability, protecting depositors, and ensuring the integrity of the banking sector. License revocation represents one of the most serious enforcement actions available under Nigerian banking law, typically reserved for institutions facing critical financial distress with no viable path to recovery."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"c7ccaab3c66c","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"367ebc54aaf0","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":""}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"33819de68013","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"79db972d00ee","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\n"},{"_key":"c54469b3b17a","_type":"span","marks":["strong"],"text":"1. Legal Framework and Regulatory Authority"},{"_key":"fc59fbcc8f53","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\nSection 12 of BOFIA 2020 grants the CBN broad powers to revoke banking licenses where financial institutions demonstrate persistent inability to meet regulatory requirements. The decision to revoke Union Homes’ license reflects the CBN’s determination that the institution’s financial challenges have reached a critical threshold with no reasonable prospect of remediation."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"7244737e7f71","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"f3b37432de73","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\n"},{"_key":"014d80bca8dc","_type":"span","marks":["strong"],"text":"Common Grounds for License Revocation"},{"_key":"712e41d0b5a7","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\nThe CBN may revoke a financial institution’s license based on any of the following circumstances:\n•Severe undercapitalization – failure to maintain minimum capital adequacy ratios required by the CBN\n•Insolvency or technical insolvency – liabilities exceeding assets with no recovery prospects\n•Persistent regulatory non-compliance – repeated failure to submit returns or comply with CBN directives\n•Unsafe or unsound banking practices – operations that jeopardize financial system stability\n•Prolonged operational inactivity – cessation of core banking activities for six months or more\n•Breach of licensing conditions – failure to fulfill obligations imposed when the license was granted"}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"2252a2d55b30","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"5cb9cb40f152","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\n"},{"_key":"67fa42e8de09","_type":"span","marks":["strong"],"text":"2. Immediate Actions for Depositors"}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"408b7dd3a790","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"dbf591dc91a0","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\n"},{"_key":"3d7c36f7fdcd","_type":"span","marks":["strong"],"text":"NDIC Liquidation Process"},{"_key":"d39635d28345","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\nFollowing license revocation, the NDIC automatically assumes the role of liquidator pursuant to Section 12(2) of BOFIA 2020 and Section 40 of the NDIC Act. The NDIC exercises all powers granted to liquidators under the Companies and Allied Matters Act (CAMA) and operates with a statutory mandate to protect depositors’ interests.\n"}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"c73fada9b746","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"ca95c14f77ec","_type":"span","marks":["strong"],"text":"Claims Settlement Priority"},{"_key":"eb7cb1a69145","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\nThe NDIC processes claims according to the following hierarchy:\n1.Depositors – receive first priority in asset distribution\n2.General creditors and investors – paid after depositor claims are satisfied\n3.Shareholders – last in priority, typically receiving no recovery"}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"c7962795721d","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"e009caa565c9","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\n"},{"_key":"4eb56d1a288d","_type":"span","marks":["strong"],"text":"Deposit Insurance Coverage"},{"_key":"b0bb3dbfc346","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\nThe NDIC provides deposit insurance coverage up to a maximum of N5,000,000 per depositor. This protection applies to all eligible deposit accounts held at Union Homes as of the revocation date.\nKey timelines:\n•Insured deposits (up to N5 million): paid within 30 days of account verification\n•Uninsured deposits (amounts exceeding N5 million): paid through liquidation dividends as assets are realized and debts recovered\nRequired Documentation\nTo facilitate verification and payment, depositors must provide:\n•Valid government-issued identification (national ID, driver’s license, international passport)\n•Proof of account ownership (bank statements, passbook, account opening documents)\n•Alternative bank account details for electronic fund transfers\n•Completed NDIC verification forms (distributed during the verification exercise)\nJoint Account Treatment"}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"34e74fe0c8ae","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"3804f3a0eee5","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\nWhere depositors hold both individual and joint accounts, each account receives separate insurance coverage up to the N5 million maximum. For example, if two spouses each maintain individual accounts and also hold a joint account, all three accounts qualify for independent protection, potentially yielding total coverage of N15 million."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"01cf831bf3a9","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"302e57a06925","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\n"},{"_key":"096a02c36b9d","_type":"span","marks":["strong"],"text":"3. Options for the Affected Institution"},{"_key":"09cca4cee258","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\nWhile license revocation represents a terminal regulatory action, affected institutions retain limited recourse options:"}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"0daa19bcb6bf","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"ae9786f2c6e4","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\n"},{"_key":"29900db0f496","_type":"span","marks":["strong"],"text":"Judicial Review"},{"_key":"c4dcb2031f35","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\nUnion Homes retains the right to seek judicial review of the CBN’s decision before the Federal High Court. However, Sections 12(4), (5), and (6) of BOFIA 2020 impose significant limitations on judicial intervention. Courts cannot compel the CBN to reinstate a revoked license, and judicial review typically focuses on procedural propriety rather than the substantive merits of the CBN’s assessment.\nLicense Reapplication\nIn cases not involving fraud or gross mismanagement, an institution may theoretically reapply for a banking license after satisfying all deficiencies that led to the initial revocation. However, given the capital requirements and operational standards imposed by current banking regulations, successful reapplication following revocation remains exceptionally rare. Institutions may alternatively seek a downgraded license for more limited financial services operations."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"3d664ce3fd8b","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"5be041e907c3","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\n"},{"_key":"49e734de3eda","_type":"span","marks":["strong"],"text":"4. Impact on Investors and Shareholders"},{"_key":"f32c28b5169a","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\nEquity investors and shareholders of Union Homes face severe consequences under the liquidation framework:\nComplete Loss of Investment\nShareholders rank last in the claims hierarchy established by Section 2(c) of the NDIC Act. In virtually all bank liquidations, asset realization proves insufficient to satisfy depositor and creditor claims, leaving nothing for distribution to shareholders. Equity investments should be considered total losses upon license revocation.\nLoss of Corporate Control\nUpon appointment as liquidator, the NDIC assumes full control over the institution’s management, assets, and operations pursuant to Section 585(9) of CAMA. The existing board of directors and management lose all decision-making authority, and shareholders cannot influence the liquidation process.\nNo Interim Distributions\nUnlike depositors who receive prompt payment of insured amounts, shareholders cannot expect any interim distributions during the liquidation process. Only after complete satisfaction of all priority claims—which may take several years—would shareholders become eligible for residual distributions, which historically have proven nonexistent in failed bank liquidations."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"213cb3ccc397","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"4405ca500470","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\n"},{"_key":"61995480070a","_type":"span","marks":["strong"],"text":"5. Broader Financial System Implications"},{"_key":"30f627388510","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\nLicense revocations generate ripple effects throughout the banking sector that extend beyond the immediate stakeholders of the failed institution:"}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"cd4bea49f25c","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"6f073a578fde","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\n"},{"_key":"12000c83e375","_type":"span","marks":["strong"],"text":"Confidence and Contagion Risk"},{"_key":"c065841d7a8e","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\nHigh-profile bank failures can erode public confidence in the broader financial system, particularly among depositors of smaller or mid-tier institutions. The CBN’s decisive action, while necessary for systemic stability, may temporarily increase deposit flight toward larger, perceived-safer institutions."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"0cb46a20adb0","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"3d5748f65ac6","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\n"},{"_key":"5cb92ed9e1e4","_type":"span","marks":["strong"],"text":"Credit Market Effects"},{"_key":"8e4668e8cfa8","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\nSurviving banks may adopt more conservative lending practices following a peer institution’s failure, potentially constraining credit availability for businesses and consumers. Interbank lending rates may also experience short-term volatility as institutions reassess counterparty risk."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"96f480e9bdfb","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"cb2af05b69ac","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\n"},{"_key":"70ed3b69db4f","_type":"span","marks":["strong"],"text":"Regulatory Precedent"},{"_key":"b62e1bee7d88","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\nThe CBN’s willingness to revoke licenses reinforces its commitment to maintaining banking sector integrity regardless of political or economic pressures. This regulatory assertiveness, while disruptive in individual cases, ultimately strengthens the long-term resilience and credibility of Nigeria’s financial system."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"49cf5aeab304","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"2a8d75d82993","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\n"},{"_key":"c98207dde6dd","_type":"span","marks":["strong"],"text":"Conclusion"},{"_key":"30cd4ed9c26f","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"\nThe revocation of Union Homes Savings & Loans PLC’s license represents a necessary, albeit painful, exercise of regulatory authority to protect depositors and maintain financial system stability. While depositors benefit from the NDIC’s insurance protection and structured liquidation process, shareholders and unsecured creditors face substantial losses.\nThe CBN’s actions underscore the critical importance of continuous regulatory compliance and sound financial management in the banking sector. Institutions operating at the margins of regulatory requirements must recognize that the CBN possesses both the authority and willingness to intervene decisively when financial stability is threatened.\nFor stakeholders affected by this revocation, timely engagement with the NDIC verification process remains essential to securing available protections and recoveries.\n\n"},{"_key":"91d96542de00","_type":"span","marks":["em"],"text":"This article is intended for informational purposes only and does not constitute legal advice. For specific guidance on matters related to Union Homes Savings & Loans PLC or other banking regulatory issues, please contact Abayomi Arole Legal Practitioners."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"}],"comments":[],"mainImage":null,"title":"Union Homes License Revocation Article"}},"__N_SSG":true}}